1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,970
In 539 BC,

2
00:00:02,170 --> 00:00:03,900
the greatest city in the ancient world fell

3
00:00:03,870 --> 00:00:06,570
to Persia. And according to one famous story,

4
00:00:06,590 --> 00:00:07,970
its walls weren't even breached.

5
00:00:08,270 --> 00:00:10,130
Babylon was built to be untouchable.

6
00:00:10,370 --> 00:00:12,230
Two giant walls. A moat.

7
00:00:12,330 --> 00:00:14,000
Gates covered in glazed blue bricks,

8
00:00:13,970 --> 00:00:15,790
guarded by dragons and bulls.

9
00:00:16,310 --> 00:00:17,920
Herodotus even claimed there was room on

10
00:00:17,890 --> 00:00:20,270
top of the walls to drive a four-horse chariot.

11
00:00:20,710 --> 00:00:22,470
Cyrus the Great had conquered a huge empire.

12
00:00:23,070 --> 00:00:24,270
Babylon was his next prize.

13
00:00:24,530 --> 00:00:26,560
The Babylonian Chronicle records that Persian

14
00:00:26,530 --> 00:00:29,220
forces defeated the Babylonian army at Opis,

15
00:00:29,190 --> 00:00:31,760
captured Sippar, and entered Babylon without

16
00:00:31,730 --> 00:00:33,170
a battle inside the city.

17
00:00:33,550 --> 00:00:36,090
Cyrus himself arrived seventeen days later.

18
00:00:36,210 --> 00:00:37,390
But about a century later,

19
00:00:37,390 --> 00:00:39,180
the Greek historian Herodotus told a very

20
00:00:39,150 --> 00:00:41,150
different story. In his version,

21
00:00:41,250 --> 00:00:43,490
Cyrus diverted the Euphrates into a marsh.

22
00:00:43,830 --> 00:00:45,400
The river through the city sank to thigh

23
00:00:45,370 --> 00:00:47,860
height. Persian soldiers waded in along

24
00:00:47,830 --> 00:00:49,730
the riverbed, right under the walls.

25
00:00:50,010 --> 00:00:51,550
And because a festival was underway,

26
00:00:51,730 --> 00:00:53,300
the people in the middle of the city didn't

27
00:00:53,270 --> 00:00:55,620
even notice. Xenophon later told a similar

28
00:00:55,590 --> 00:00:58,020
tale. But here's the twist: the contemporary

29
00:00:57,990 --> 00:01:00,600
Babylonian record never mentions this clever

30
00:01:00,570 --> 00:01:02,660
river trick. It says the city was taken

31
00:01:02,630 --> 00:01:04,840
without a battle. So did Babylon fall to

32
00:01:04,810 --> 00:01:06,310
a brilliant military trick...

33
00:01:06,490 --> 00:01:08,430
or was the real story much simpler?
